A tiny npm package for permanent state management, built around what I call
the StateFile architecture: instead of tables, rows and query languages, your
application state lives in substates — files of typed data units that load,
update and persist with plain function calls.

# How it works

A `StateManager` scans a root directory and loads your state; each substate is
a `StateFile` you `add` units to, retrieve with `get`/`getWhere`, and change
through builder functions in `update`.

- Declared types, enforced integrity: `extendUnitType` declares which fields
of a data unit are updatable, so updates can't silently touch undeclared
fields. It also supports nested objects and array fields.
- Passkeys: a `StateFile` can be given a passkey, and deleting it physically
requires that key.
- Cracks: `seal`, `split`, `loadOne` and `unloadOne` slice a substate into
pieces so you can keep hot data in RAM and cold data on disk.

The whole design is written up in
[StateFile: a minimalist design for permanent state management](https://dev.to/_moehab/statefile-a-minimalist-design-for-permanent-state-management-43d7).
